    "knowledge_point": "Solving a System of Equations Using Back Substitution Given an Augmented Matrix",
    "problem": "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{3} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
    "worked_solution": "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say,  ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=2 \\\\  & {x}_{2}+\\frac{{x}_{3}}{3}=-1 \\\\  & {x}_{3}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+\\frac{2}{3}=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-1-\\frac{2}{3}=-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{3}$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+3(-\\frac{5}{3})+(2)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2+5-2=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=5$, ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{3}$, and ${x}_{3}=2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨5,-\\frac{5}{3},2⟩.$",
